    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **James Wood** stands as our premier play with a 78.099 score, bolstered significantly by a favorable matchup against Bratt (5.90 xERA) and the elite stolen base environment created by his speed profile. **Austin Riley** also presents a high-floor profile at 73.323, largely due to a +16% park adjustment that optimizes his power potential despite hitting lower in the order. We are also leaning heavily on **Brice Turang** (71.090) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (68.831), both of whom benefit from significant pitcher-skill advantages against Feltner and Rojas, respectively. **Corbin Carroll** remains a must-start at 66.252; while facing a respectable Griffin, his elite speed profile is currently amplified by a +4% boost from the opposing pitcher\u2019s delivery cadence, which should provide the necessary runway for our stolen base targets.\n\nOur most difficult bench decision involved **Josh Naylor**, who missed the starting cut with a 53.396 score. While his base projection remained solid, the matchup against the high-skill Eovaldi (3.42 xERA) penalized his efficiency enough to keep him behind the more favorable situational alignments of **Isaac Paredes** and **Travis Bazzana**. We continue to monitor **Henry Bolte**, who sits at 59.637; while he remains a risky play in the 8-hole, his high-upside platoon advantage against Rojas gives him the edge over **Edwin Arroyo** and **Seiya Suzuki**, the latter of whom is effectively neutralized by the presence of a top-tier arm in Skenes.\n\nInvestors should remain cautious regarding the matchup between **Gabriel Moreno** and **Corbin Carroll** against Griffin, as the 3.68 xERA presents the most significant hurdle on the board today. **Jose Caballero** remains a marginal play at 48.354, primarily due to an aggressive 1.86s pop-time from the opposing catcher which suppresses his expected stolen base efficiency. With **Zach McKinstry** also floating near our floor at 49.761, we will be strictly monitoring these spots to ensure we preserve our game caps if early-inning production fails to materialize.",
